# Find Pivot Index — LeetCode 724

**Pattern:** Prefix Sum

## Approach
- Calculate the total sum of the array.
- Maintain `leftSum`, initially `0`.
- For each index `i`:
  - Calculate the right sum as `totalSum - leftSum - nums[i]`.
  - If `leftSum == rightSum`, return `i`.
  - Otherwise, add `nums[i]` to `leftSum`.
- If no pivot index is found, return `-1`.

## Java Code

```java
class Solution {
    public int pivotIndex(int[] nums) {
        int totalSum = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {
            totalSum += num;
        }

        int leftSum = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int rightSum = totalSum - leftSum - nums[i];

            if (leftSum == rightSum) {
                return i;
            }

            leftSum += nums[i];
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

## Dry Run

**Input:** `nums = [1, 7, 3, 6, 5, 6]`

Total sum = `28`

| Index | Current value | Left sum | Right sum | Result |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| 0 | 1 | 0 | 27 | No |
| 1 | 7 | 1 | 20 | No |
| 2 | 3 | 8 | 17 | No |
| 3 | 6 | 11 | 11 | Pivot found |

**Output:** `3`

At index `3`, the left sum is `1 + 7 + 3 = 11`, and the right sum is `5 + 6 = 11`.

## Complexity
- **Time:** \(O(n)\) — two passes through the array.
- **Space:** \(O(1)\) — only a few variables are used.

## Key Takeaways
- `leftSum` contains elements strictly before the current index.
- `rightSum = totalSum - leftSum - nums[i]`.
- Check each index from left to right to return the **leftmost pivot index**.
- Edge indices are valid pivots when the sum on the existing side is `0`.
